oaxaca-base (0.1.8) stonking; urgency=medium

  * The thunderbolt driver waits for the GPU driver's DPRX capability read
    without a time limit (/usr/lib/modprobe.d/oaxaca-thunderbolt.conf,
    options thunderbolt dprx_timeout=-1, copied into the initramfs). By
    default it gives up after 12 s and tears the DisplayPort tunnel down;
    i915 loads only after the disk encryption passphrase, so a slow
    passphrase left a Thunderbolt display dark until replugged.
  * Drop 0.1.7's initramfs module list: loading the thunderbolt driver
    early did not change the timeout, which starts when the driver sets up
    the tunnel.

 -- santisbon <santisbon@users.noreply.github.com>  Thu, 15 Oct 2026 18:30:00 -0500

oaxaca-base (0.1.7) stonking; urgency=medium

  * The initramfs loads the thunderbolt driver at its start
    (/usr/share/initramfs-tools/modules.d/oaxaca-thunderbolt), before the
    disk encryption passphrase prompt. Loaded only after the disk was
    unlocked, the driver met an inactive firmware DisplayPort tunnel when
    the passphrase took 20 s or more (11 s was still fine), tore it down, and the Apple
    Studio Display stayed dark until replugged. The package activates
    update-initramfs.

 -- santisbon <santisbon@users.noreply.github.com>  Thu, 15 Oct 2026 17:30:00 -0500

oaxaca-base (0.1.6) stonking; urgency=medium

  * apt installs Recommends again, as a stock Ubuntu does: drop
    /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/50oaxaca-no-recommends (removed from existing
    systems through dpkg-maintscript-helper rm_conffile). Every test round
    found another package a stock Ubuntu gets through Recommends; turning
    them on costs about 1 GB installed.
  * Ship /etc/apt/preferences.d/oaxaca-blocked-recommends, apt pins
    that keep out two Recommends: xdg-desktop-portal-gnome (Files
    recommends it, and it recommends gnome-shell, which would bring GNOME
    Shell, gdm3, the Ubuntu GNOME session and IBus) and sudo-rs (sudo
    recommends it, and its /usr/bin/sudo alternative outranks classic
    sudo's, whose regular-expression sudoers rules the menus use).

 -- santisbon <santisbon@users.noreply.github.com>  Wed, 14 Oct 2026 21:00:00 -0500

oaxaca-base (0.1.5) stonking; urgency=medium

  * Depend on what stonking's systemd and udev recommend and a stock Ubuntu
    therefore installs, skipped here because Recommends are off: the TPM
    tools (systemd-cryptsetup, which carries systemd-cryptenroll;
    systemd-tpm, tpm-udev, libtss2-rc0t64), systemd-hwe-hwdb (Ubuntu's
    updated hardware database), libnss-systemd and linux-sysctl-defaults.
    TPM unlock of the disk is not turned on by this (docs/7 choice 47).

 -- santisbon <santisbon@users.noreply.github.com>  Tue, 13 Oct 2026 23:30:00 -0500

oaxaca-base (0.1.4) stonking; urgency=medium

  * Depend on fwupd and fwupd-signed, so every machine gets firmware
    updates: fwupd only recommends fwupd-signed, the Canonical-signed EFI
    program that applies UEFI capsule updates (BIOS/UEFI firmware), and
    Recommends are off here, so without it no system firmware update could
    be applied, with Secure Boot on or off.

